{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And the son of a woman went out.</b> According to the plain sense, the language of going out is appropriate in connection with a quarrel, as it is written: ״יצאו נצבים״, ״אל תצא לריב מהר״. Here Rashi explained: they said to him — a man by his banner with signs, according to his fathers' house, as it is written; he entered the court of Moses and went out liable. And if you ask: why did he go out liable? Was not the gatherer of wood in the first year, as is said in general: had Israel observed two Sabbaths, no nation or tongue could rule over them — they observed the first, and in the second this one came and profaned it; and so Rashi explained in the portion of Shelach, and likewise the blasphemer too came in that same episode immediately — if so, why did this one go out liable? Was not the pericope of the banners not stated until the second year, as written in the portion of BeMidbar? Rather, one may say: even so, from when they came to the wilderness of Sinai they were encamped in the order that our father Jacob transmitted to them, as Rashi explained in the portion of VaYehi and in the portion of BeMidbar, and according to the arrangement of the order he went out liable." "<b>And he was the son of an Egyptian man.</b> Even though he was not a mamzer at that time, he was like a mamzer." "<b>In the midst of the children of Israel.</b> Rashi explained: this teaches that he converted. And if you ask: is it not stated above in the portion of Shemot, where Rashi explained ״וירא כי אין איש״ — that no one was destined to convert from him? One may answer: when Moses killed the Egyptian, Shelomit was pregnant from him, and Moses saw through the holy spirit that no descendants would emerge from him from that point onward who would convert, so he killed him. And furthermore, we find no merit adduced on behalf of the blasphemer anywhere. This teaches that he converted. And if you ask: why was conversion necessary? Is it not established that a slave or a Gentile who has relations with a Jewish woman — the offspring is valid? Rather, one may answer: we say 'the offspring is valid' — that applies after the giving of the Torah, where lineage follows the mother, and she is a Jewess. But here the Egyptian had relations with her before the giving of the Torah, when she was a daughter of Noah, and the offspring is not valid, and lineage follows the father, so he was an Egyptian — therefore he required conversion. Another interpretation: <b>in the midst of the children of Israel.</b> According to the plain sense, he was attached to his Egyptian father from the time of the enslavement until after the giving of the Torah, and afterward he converted and came to his mother's family."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:14:59.929171838Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}